5, 0.019439697265625, 0.0016069412231445312, 0.0140838623046875, 0.035491943359375, -0.00777435302734375, -0.0007829666137695312, -0.0133209228515625, 0.02154541015625, 0.0209197998046875, 0.051483154296875, 0.0008087158203125, 0.0205230712890625, -0.03387451171875, -0.0273284912109375, 0.0254669189453125, -0.0200042724609375, 0.02490234375, 0.00505828857421875, -0.047698974609375, -0.019073486328125, 0.00490570068359375, 0.0012340545654296875, 0.0166015625, 0.03607177734375, -0.01216888427734375, 0.001651763916015625, 0.00015854835510253906, 0.0042724609375, 0.006755828857421875, -0.0035610198974609375, 0.00762176513671875, -0.017303466796875, -0.016845703125, 0.00487518310546875, -0.0177001953125, 0.00405120849609375, -0.006092071533203125, -0.0102691650390625, 0.038543701171875, -0.02838134765625, -0.00917816162109375, 0.0060577392578125, 0.0164794921875, 0.0167083740234375, -0.045257568359375, -0.00504302978515625, 0.014007568359375], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Delegating cognitive operations to generative artificial intelligence (AI) changes how tasks are executed and raises a governance question: at what point does human direction shape the outcome? This article examines two main ways humans guide AI tasks. First, **specified delegation** refers to setting instructions, constraints, or criteria before the AI begins\u2014clearly defining the task in advance, such as providing a detailed prompt or explicit requirements. For example, a manager might instruct an AI to generate a report using a specific data set and format, with all parameters set before the AI starts. Second, **iterative coproduction** involves ongoing human intervention during the AI\u2019s work, where humans review, correct, or redirect outputs as they are produced; for example, by giving feedback or making adjustments after each AI-generated draft, such as a writer refining an article through multiple rounds of AI suggestions and edits.\n\nTo analyze these approaches, we use data from the Anthropic Economic Index (AEI) for April and May 2026. The AEI is a composite measure developed by Anthropic to track and quantify patterns of human-AI interaction across various domains. It aggregates monthly activity data from multiple sources, categorizing interactions by context (such as work or personal use) and by the mode of engagement. The AEI\u2019s methodology involves collecting anonymized usage logs, classifying tasks, and quantifying delegation profiles based on observable interaction patterns. While the AEI provides a broad and systematic view of human-AI collaboration, it is limited by its reliance on usage logs, which may not capture all nuances of user intent or context. The index\u2019s reliability is supported by cross-validation with independent usage surveys, but its validity is constrained by the granularity of available metadata and potential biases in self-reported task categories.\n\nThe study distinguishes between two modes of use: **1P API** and **Claude.ai**. The 1P API (First-Party Application Programming Interface) is a technical interface that allows developers and organizations to integrate Anthropic\u2019s AI models directly into their own software or workflows, typically for structured or automated tasks. For instance, a company might use the 1P API to automate customer support responses based on predefined rules. In contrast, **Claude.ai** is a user-facing platform that includes both Chat and Cowork environments, supporting a broader range of interactive and conversational uses, from brainstorming to document drafting, where users can iteratively refine outputs in real time.\n\nOur study investigates whether a stronger work-related orientation in human-AI interaction is linked to more specified delegation within each mode, and whether iterative coproduction increases more in Claude.ai than in 1P API. We focus on **level-0 O*NET tasks**, which are broad occupational activities defined by the Occupational Information Network (O*NET) taxonomy. These tasks represent high-level job functions (such as \u201canalyze data\u201d or \u201ccommunicate information\u201d) and provide a standardized basis for comparing human-AI collaboration across different occupations. Level-0 tasks were chosen because they capture the most generalizable and widely shared aspects of work, allowing for consistent comparison across diverse domains and minimizing confounding from highly specialized or idiosyncratic activities.\n\nThe main analysis estimates how both delegation profiles change when an eligible record reallocates ten percentage points from personal use to work-related use. Eligible node-month pairs were selected based on their presence and eligibility in both modes, resulting in 1,411 pairs for the iterative comparison. Statistical analysis was conducted using linear regression models, controlling for node and month fixed effects, to estimate the average change in delegation profiles and to compute 95% confidence intervals for the observed effects. The models assume linearity and independence of observations, but potential biases may arise from unobserved confounders or misclassification of task context. Robustness checks included alternative model specifications and sensitivity analyses to assess the stability of results.\n\nResults show that specified delegation increases by 2.76 points (95% CI: [2.30, 3.22]) in 1P API and by 1.45 points (95% CI: [0.93, 1.97]) in Claude.ai when work-related use rises. On the common support, iterative coproduction changes by \u20130.30 points in 1P API and by 0.15 points in Claude.ai, yielding a between-mode difference of 0.45 points (95% CI: [0.15, 0.75]). These findings suggest that as work-related use increases, organizations tend to rely more on up-front specification of tasks, especially in technical integration settings like 1P API, where automation and compliance are prioritized. In contrast, interactive platforms like Claude.ai see a modest increase in ongoing human involvement, reflecting the need for flexibility and adaptation in creative or collaborative tasks. The between-mode difference indicates that the context of use\u2014whether technical integration or interactive engagement\u2014not only shapes the prevalence but also the nature of human intervention. Specifically, more technical settings (1P API) are associated with clearer traces of prior human direction, while interactive platforms (Claude.ai) facilitate more ongoing, iterative involvement. These patterns may be driven by the affordances of each platform: APIs are optimized for automation and repeatability, whereas user-facing interfaces support dynamic, conversational workflows.\n\nIn summary, this article shifts attention from the quantity of AI execution to the timing and visibility of human direction. By clarifying when and how humans guide generative AI, we highlight important differences in governance and oversight across modes of use. These insights suggest that policy and organizational practices should consider not just how much AI is used, but also the forms and timing of human involvement. For example, organizations deploying AI via APIs should ensure robust up-front specification and documentation of task requirements, while those using interactive platforms should implement processes for ongoing review and feedback. Such tailored approaches can help ensure accountability and effective oversight in diverse AI deployment contexts."}}